CAT = "s7Funds"; SEG = "FND"
Q = [
("A mutual fund has a NAV of $19.00 and a public offering price of $20.00. Its sales charge as a percentage of the offering price is:",
 "5%", ["5.26%", "1%", "4.75%"],
 "Sales charge % = (POP - NAV) / POP = $1 / $20 = 5%. Dividing by NAV gives 5.26%, which is the wrong base."),
("A customer signs a letter of intent to reach a fund's $50,000 breakpoint. The letter covers purchases made within:",
 "13 months", ["12 months", "90 days", "24 months"],
 "A letter of intent gives the breakpoint price on purchases over 13 months, and it may be backdated up to 90 days to include a recent purchase."),
("A rep recommends buying $48,000 of a fund just below its $50,000 breakpoint without mentioning it. This is:",
 "Breakpoint selling, a violation", ["Acceptable if fully disclosed later", "Acceptable for B shares", "Required for suitability"],
 "Selling just below a breakpoint, or failing to tell the customer about it, so the customer pays a higher sales charge is a prohibited practice."),
("Rights of accumulation let a fund investor:",
 "Count holdings toward breakpoints", ["Buy more shares at NAV for life", "Reinvest gains at the offering price", "Skip delivery of a new prospectus"],
 "Under rights of accumulation, the value of shares already owned in the fund family counts toward the next breakpoint on new purchases."),
("Which mutual fund share class usually suits a long-term investor buying a large amount at once?",
 "Class A shares", ["Class C shares", "Class B shares", "Class R shares"],
 "Class A shares carry a front-end load that drops at breakpoints and lower ongoing 12b-1 fees, so they are usually cheapest for large, long-held purchases."),
("A customer plans to hold a fund for about two years. Which share class is usually most cost-effective?",
 "Class C shares", ["Class A shares", "Class B shares", "Any class equally"],
 "C shares have little or no front load and a short CDSC, but higher ongoing 12b-1 fees, so they suit short holding periods. Over many years A shares usually cost less."),
("Under FINRA rules, a fund may describe itself as no-load only if its 12b-1 fee is no more than:",
 "0.25% a year", ["1.00% a year", "0.75% a year", "0.50% a year"],
 "A fund with no front or back load and a 12b-1 fee of 0.25% or less may call itself no-load. The maximum 12b-1 fee is 1%, of which up to 0.25% is a service fee."),
("Mutual fund orders received before the 4 p.m. ET close are priced at:",
 "That day's next-calculated NAV", ["The prior day's NAV", "The opening NAV that day", "The price shown when the order is entered"],
 "Forward pricing means an order is filled at the next price computed after it is received, normally that day's closing NAV plus any sales charge."),
("An investor redeems open-end fund shares. The fund must pay the proceeds within:",
 "Seven calendar days", ["One business day", "Thirty calendar days", "Ten business days"],
 "The Investment Company Act requires an open-end fund to pay redemption proceeds within seven calendar days of receiving the request."),
("A customer switches from one fund to another fund in the same family with no new sales charge. For tax purposes the switch is:",
 "A taxable sale and purchase", ["Not a taxable event", "Taxed only on dividends", "Deferred until the new fund is sold"],
 "An exchange within a fund family avoids a new load but is still a sale of the first fund, so any gain or loss is realized."),
("Which describes an exchange-traded fund?",
 "Trades all day at market prices", ["Priced once a day at net asset value", "Always sold with a front load", "Has a fixed number of shares"],
 "ETFs trade on exchanges throughout the day and can be bought on margin or sold short. Open-end mutual funds are priced once a day."),
("A customer holds a 2x daily leveraged ETF for six months. The index ends flat after large daily swings. The fund's return is most likely:",
 "Negative", ["Exactly zero", "Positive", "Double the gain"],
 "Daily resets compound returns, and in a volatile market that ends flat the losses from compounding leave a leveraged fund below where it started. This is why such funds suit short holding periods."),
("A unit investment trust is best described as:",
 "A fixed portfolio, not actively managed", ["An actively managed portfolio", "A closed-end fund traded on exchanges", "A subaccount of a variable annuity"],
 "A UIT buys a set portfolio and holds it until termination, with no investment adviser making ongoing changes. Units are redeemable with the trust."),
("In a variable annuity, the value of an accumulation unit changes with:",
 "The separate account's performance", ["The insurer's general account returns", "The fixed guaranteed rate", "The annuitant's age"],
 "During accumulation, units rise and fall with the subaccounts in the separate account. A fixed annuity's value comes from the general account."),
("A variable annuity's AIR is 5%. The separate account earns 7% one month and 4% the next. Compared with the prior payment, the payment after the second month will:",
 "Decrease", ["Increase", "Stay the same", "Return to the original amount"],
 "Each payment is compared with the one before it: returns above the AIR raise it and returns below lower it. A 4% month, below the 5% AIR, reduces the next payment."),
("Withdrawals from a nonqualified deferred annuity are taxed:",
 "Earnings first, as ordinary income", ["Principal first, tax free", "Pro rata, as long-term capital gains", "Only the death benefit"],
 "Before annuitization, withdrawals are treated as last in, first out, so earnings come out first and are taxed as ordinary income, plus a 10% penalty before 59 1/2 unless an exception applies."),
("A 1035 exchange lets a customer:",
 "Swap one annuity for another tax free", ["Withdraw all of the annuity's gains tax free", "Roll an IRA into a 401(k)", "Avoid surrender charges"],
 "A Section 1035 exchange defers tax on a like-kind switch between annuity contracts, but the new contract may start a fresh surrender period, which must be weighed in a recommendation."),
("Which statement about direct participation programs is true?",
 "Income and losses pass to investors", ["They pay tax at the entity level", "They are usually highly liquid", "Losses offset any type of income"],
 "Limited partnerships are pass-through entities reported on a K-1. Their passive losses generally offset only passive income, and units are usually illiquid."),
("A limited partner in a DPP:",
 "Cannot take part in management", ["Manages the daily operations", "Has unlimited liability", "Owes duties to other partners"],
 "Limited partners provide capital and have liability limited to their investment, but lose that protection if they take part in management. The general partner manages."),
("Which DPP is designed mainly for tax credits and is government assisted?",
 "Low-income housing program", ["Raw land program", "Oil and gas exploratory program", "Equipment leasing program"],
 "Government-assisted housing programs provide tax credits and losses, with little cash flow or appreciation expected. Raw land offers appreciation but no deductions."),
("A 529 plan's earnings are tax free when withdrawn for:",
 "Qualified education expenses", ["Any expense after age 59 1/2", "A down payment on a home", "Expenses of any family member"],
 "Withdrawals for qualified education costs are tax free federally. Nonqualified withdrawals are taxed on earnings and usually carry a 10% penalty."),
("A municipal fund securities product sold to fund college costs is a:",
 "529 savings plan", ["Coverdell savings account", "UTMA account", "Roth IRA"],
 "529 savings plans are municipal fund securities under MSRB rules because a state sponsors them. Coverdell ESAs are trust or custodial accounts, not municipal securities."),
("Compared with an ETF holding the same index, an exchange-traded note adds:",
 "The issuer's credit risk", ["Daily NAV pricing", "Unlimited liability", "Mandatory annual payouts"],
 "An ETN is an unsecured debt note of the issuing bank, so its value depends on the issuer's credit as well as the index. An ETF owns the underlying securities."),
("Which is true of a nontraded REIT?",
 "Shares can be hard to sell", ["Shares trade on an exchange", "It pays no distributions", "It is exempt from FINRA rules"],
 "Nontraded REITs are registered but not listed, so liquidity is limited and redemption programs may be suspended. Fees and valuation are key disclosure points."),
]
